---

**Chapter 4: Rising Above**

Aron squinted up at the steel skeleton that clawed at the sky, sunlight glinting
off beams that soared higher than he’d ever imagined. The new towers dwarfed 
every other building in the city, their blueprints ambitious, their promise 
undeniable. He wiped the sweat from his brow and looked down at his clipboard.

“Hey, Aron!” Maya’s voice rang out over the din of jackhammers and welding 
torches. “You’re going to want to see this.”

He made his way across the construction site, dodging cables and stacks of glass
panels. Maya stood by the edge of the platform, her hard hat tipped back, eyes 
wide with awe. Beyond her, a crane hoisted a massive steel beam into place—one 
of the final pieces for the hundred-and-fifty-fifth floor.

“Can you believe it?” Maya grinned, her voice trembling with excitement. “When I
was a kid, I used to stare at the old towers from the ferry. Now look at this. 
Taller than anything we’ve ever built.”

Aron chuckled. “I can believe it. I’ve been up here every day for two years. But
it still takes my breath away.”

A deep laugh rumbled behind them. It was Mr. Okoye, the site manager, clipboard 
in hand, glasses fogged with the morning humidity. “Don’t get too sentimental, 
you two. We’ve still got another thirty floors to go before we top out.”

Maya nudged Aron. “He’s pretending he’s not impressed, but yesterday I caught 
him taking selfies with the new spire.”

Mr. Okoye shook his head, a smile breaking through. “All right, all right. But 
seriously—look at those supports. Triple-reinforced steel. This building isn’t 
just tall. It’s a fortress.”

Aron’s gaze followed the lattice of beams and cables. “We promised the city 
stronger, and that’s what we’re giving them. I ran the numbers myself—these 
towers could take anything thrown at them.”

A gust of wind rattled the scaffolding, and Aron felt a thrill run through him. 
This was more than construction. It was a statement. A promise that the city 
would always rise again.

Suddenly, Aron’s phone buzzed. He checked the message and frowned. “Looks like 
the governor’s here. Wants to see the progress.”

Maya whistled. “Big day.”

Mr. Okoye clapped Aron on the shoulder. “Go on, show her what we’ve built. Tell 
her we’re not just making history—we’re making the future.”

Aron nodded, tucking his clipboard under his arm. He took one last look up at 
the soaring frame above him. The new Twin Towers—taller, stronger, bigger. A 
symbol, reborn.

He smiled. “Let’s show them what New York can do.”

